<section id="A6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.1.3 </span>Font Style</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Style recognition remains challenging, with the best model achieving only 33.6%. The confusion matrix in Table <a href="#A6.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ F.1.2 Font Size ‣ F.1 Per-Property Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix F Detailed Analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> reveals a dominant “regular” bias: the model predicts regular for 67.6–80.0% of <em id="A6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> samples regardless of true style, including 70.5% of bold and 80.0% of italic samples. Bold-italic is essentially never predicted (0.0% for three of four true styles). This extreme bias suggests that VLMs largely ignore stylistic visual cues and default to the most common style, treating font style as invisible metadata rather than a perceivable visual attribute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.1.4 </span>Font Color</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Color recognition achieves near-perfect accuracy for most models, with most achieving <math id="A6.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>89% and most exceeding 97%. Gemini-3-Flash is flawless at 100% across all eight colors (Table <a href="#A6.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ F.1.4 Font Color ‣ F.1 Per-Property Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix F Detailed Analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Qwen2.5-VL-32B is a notable exception at 89.2%, with consistent errors across all colors, suggesting a systematic issue in its color pipeline rather than difficulty with specific hues.</p>
</div>
